# PHASE 1 — ADMIN AUTHENTICATION & MODERATOR MANAGEMENT

## 1A. Secure Admin Authentication

Inspect the existing admin login, dashboard, authorization logic, Worker APIs and environment variables.

Identify the exact missing configuration or implementation before making changes.

### Requirements

- Preserve the existing admin interface and application architecture wherever possible.
- Use the existing authentication system if it can securely support administrative access.
- If dedicated admin credentials are necessary, validate them exclusively on the server.
- Store sensitive credentials using the appropriate Cloudflare Worker secret mechanism.
- Never hardcode production credentials in source code.
- Never expose admin passwords, password hashes, session secrets or privileged Supabase keys to the browser.
- Use secure password handling and constant-time comparison where applicable.
- Apply appropriate session security, including expiration, secure cookies where supported, HttpOnly, Secure and suitable SameSite settings.
- Protect state-changing operations against CSRF where the authentication architecture requires it.
- Rate-limit repeated failed login attempts where practical.
- Ensure logout invalidates the server-side session or otherwise prevents continued use of revoked sessions.
- Do not rely on frontend route guards as the only authorization mechanism.

### Admin authorization

Verify that every admin API and privileged operation checks authorization on the server.

A hidden button, protected React route or client-side role value is not sufficient.

Inspect:

- Admin dashboard APIs.
- Admin user and role management.
- Moderator assignment and removal.
- AI participant configuration and controls.
- Any other existing privileged endpoints.

Use least-privilege access. Do not give guests, ordinary members or moderators unrestricted admin permissions.

### Required tests

- Correct admin credentials.
- Incorrect username.
- Incorrect password.
- Missing credentials.
- Logout and subsequent access.
- Expired or invalid session.
- Direct access to admin routes without authorization.
- Direct API requests without authorization.
- Attempts by an ordinary member, guest and moderator to perform admin-only operations.
- Session and cookie security where applicable.

Never reveal real credentials in the test report.

## 1B. Moderator Management

