## 3C. AI Chat Participants

Inspect the existing AI participant implementation, if any, before building or modifying it.

The goal is to provide clearly identified AI chat characters that can participate naturally in eligible public chatrooms.

### Character plan

The intended initial target is up to 20 configured AI characters per eligible room:

- 15 female-presenting characters.
- 5 male-presenting characters.

This is a configuration target, not permission to create 20 continuously running background agents in every room.

Determine whether the current architecture can support the target safely and affordably. If not, explain the practical limits and propose a controlled implementation.

### Identity and transparency

- Every AI participant must be clearly identified as AI.
- Never represent an AI character as a real human or genuine human moderator.
- Never include AI participants in genuine human online counts.
- Keep AI identities separate from registered member accounts.
- Give each character a stable identity and configurable display name/personality.
- Do not claim a character is online unless its state is actually active.

### Conversation behavior

- Allow administrators to enable or disable AI participation globally and per room, where supported.
- Avoid repetitive messages, flooding and excessive simultaneous responses.
- Use controlled response timing and message frequency.
- Prioritize real user messages over unnecessary AI chatter.
- Prevent characters from replying indefinitely to one another.
- Avoid duplicate responses and self-triggering loops.
- Apply message length, frequency and cost limits.
- Respect room isolation and the existing chat moderation system.
- Avoid unsolicited private messages to real members unless a separately authorized feature is explicitly designed and approved.

### Admin controls

Where feasible within the existing architecture, provide controls for:

- Global AI enable/disable.
- Per-room AI enable/disable.
- Character activation and deactivation.
- Character display names and personalities.
- Response frequency or other safe limits.
- Usage and cost monitoring.
- Provider configuration status.

All privileged controls must use server-side authorization.

### Provider and runtime requirements

- Inspect the existing AI provider integration before choosing or adding a provider.
- Store provider API keys only in server-side secrets.
- Never place private AI provider credentials in frontend code or public `VITE_*` variables.
- Do not invent API keys or claim a provider is configured when it is not.
- If a provider secret or account configuration is missing, document the exact variable name and required setup without exposing its value.
- Use bounded, event-driven processing appropriate to Cloudflare Workers.
- Do not assume a Worker can run an unlimited background loop or remain active indefinitely.
- Implement appropriate timeouts, concurrency limits, error handling and cost controls.
- Avoid unnecessary paid API calls during development and testing.
- Do not allow AI participants to bypass existing moderation or safety controls.

### AI verification

